 ["A name that cannot be recalled at a meeting comes back later when the person sees the name\'s first letter. Which kind of failure was it?","retrieval failure","encoding failure","storage decay","The memory was stored but could not be reached until a cue appeared."],
 ["How do most memory researchers view the claim that traumatic memories are commonly repressed and later recovered accurately?","with caution, because the evidence is disputed and recovered memories can be inaccurate","as firmly established by laboratory experiments","as proven by Ebbinghaus\'s forgetting curve","The idea of repression is controversial, and research shows that suggestive techniques can produce false memories. Traumatic events are often remembered rather than forgotten."],
 ["What does Ebbinghaus\'s forgetting curve show about material memorized and not reviewed?","loss is fastest soon after learning and then levels off","loss is equal each day","nothing is lost for the first week","The classic finding is a steep early drop followed by slower loss, though exact values depend on the material and method."],
 ["After surgery, the patient known as H.M. could not form new explicit memories but could still learn some motor skills. What does this describe?","anterograde amnesia","retrograde amnesia","infantile amnesia","Anterograde amnesia is difficulty forming new memories. H.M.\'s spared skill learning supports the distinction between explicit and implicit memory."],
 ["After a head injury, a person cannot remember events from the year before the injury. Which condition is this?","retrograde amnesia","anterograde amnesia","source amnesia","Retrograde amnesia is loss of memories for events before the injury."],
 ["Most adults can recall almost nothing from their first two years of life. What is this called?","infantile amnesia","retroactive interference","anterograde amnesia","Infantile (childhood) amnesia is the scarcity of memories from early life, and its causes are still studied."],
 ["A person remembers a fact but wrongly believes a doctor told them, when it came from a television show. Which error is this?","source amnesia","proactive interference","anterograde amnesia","Source amnesia, or source misattribution, is remembering information but forgetting where it came from."],
 ["Witnesses asked how fast cars were going when they smashed into each other gave higher estimates than those asked about cars that hit each other. Which phenomenon does this illustrate?","the misinformation effect","state-dependent memory","the serial position effect","Wording changed the memory report, showing that later information can alter recollection of an event."],
 ["In studies that suggested a false childhood event to adults, some participants came to report it as a real memory. What does this suggest?","memory is reconstructive and some people can form false memories","all childhood memories are accurate","false memories occur only after brain damage","False memories can appear after suggestion, but only a minority of participants typically do so."]
